1 Goals
The course project provides an exciting opportunity to:
• Conduct independent research in NLP. You will choose a paper or a couple of papers of your choice
and focus on some of its aspects: either a possible extension, a related or modified model, a novel
evaluation method, etc. Alternatively, you can choose a topic you are interested in and expand the
scientific knowledge about it.
• Potentially contribute to science. While it would be great if your project yields new results, findings,
or resources (in which case we will consider pursuing publication in an upcoming NLP conference),
to get full credit it is sufficient to describe what you wanted to achieve, how you tried to achieve it,
and what were the challenges you faced, as well as how it fits into the existing knowledge around
this topic.
• Experience research and the joy of new discoveries!
2 The Project Proposal
The goal of the project proposal is to let us know what you plan to do for your project, how you plan
to do it, and where it fits into the existing state of knowledge and science in NLP. It is mandatory, and
you will get a binary yes/no approval for it (no numerical grade), depending on whether the proposal is
clear enough and deemed appropriate and feasible for the class.
Choose your groups: You can work in groups of up to four students. Larger groups require case-bycase
approval in advance and are expected to propose a more ambitious, broader-scope project. Working
in teams may involve some logistical efforts, but it is often worth the tradeoff, as more teammates means
more working hands and more minds to brainstorm with. Sign up for project groups on Moodle.
Identify key paper(s): choose at least one and no more than three research papers relevant to
your proposed project and explain this in your proposal. Include full bibliography references to those
papers. Focus on papers that have been published in top-tier NLP/ML venues ideally after 2018. Other
papers may still be appropriate in some cases. See more details in Section 6.1.
