6.1 Research paper resources
The number and variety of research papers in NLP and ML today are difficult to navigate. When
choosing papers to focus on, it is best to focus on more recent work (say, later than 2018) and that has
been published in a top-tier peer-reviewed venue in the field. Many great papers do not fall into this
category. If you are interested in a particular paper or venue, but you are unsure if it is legit and reliable
to base your project on, you can email me to consult.
While it is impossible to list here all prestigious venues where great NLP papers might be published, here
are a few good ones:
• EMNLP, ACL, NAACL: See here. These are venues specialized to NLP. The list contains many
other NLP venues, not all of the same tier.
• NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR: These are top-tier general ML (and beyond) venues. They will have papers
on all topics in ML, you will need to filter for NLP.
Each of these venues publishes many hundreds of papers yearly, so you would need to navigate them using
searches, keywords, topic filters, tools like Google Scholar and Semantic Scholar, and so on. Papers from
these venues will generally be openly available online on the venue’s website and/or on paper repositories
like arXiv.
You may choose to use generative AI to point you to key papers on your topics of interest. It can provide
good pointers. However, be wary of all the usual risks and pitfalls of relying on AI, and particularly
hallucinations (non-existent papers, or papers that exist but the AI hallucinates their content when
describing it to you); they still happen, even in the most advanced frontier models.
6.2 Model suites
Model suites like OLMo and Pythia include pre-trained/fine-tuned models and their training data, often
with sequence-level metadata information (e.g., which sequences the model saw during each step of
training). This facilitates studying various problems in LLMs and NLP. You can think of it as a playground
where we can train, intervene, and analyze small-scale language models with full visibility into
their training data. Keep in mind that these models are not as performant as frontier LLMs (they are
pre-trained, but not fine-tuned or aligned in any way, and were trained on little data). Still, as frontier
LLMs rely on similar principles and architectures and their pre-training process is (likely) similar, some
conclusions may transfer.
Even if you choose to work with these suites, be sure to carefully consider scope and feasibility; some of
their models are still too large to fit and run on available class resources.
