Mentored project proposal: Understanding Entity Binding Mechanisms
Mentor: PhD student Yoav Gur Arieh
A key component of in-context reasoning is the ability of language models (LMs) to bind entities for
later retrieval. For example, an LM might represent Ann loves pie by binding Ann to pie, allowing it
to later retrieve Ann when asked Who loves pie? We found that LMs implement this using three types
of pointer-based mechanisms: positional, lexical and reflexive. However, there still remain many open
questions:
• What are the components that implement these mechanisms in LMs? Can we precisely identify
a circuit that implements them? Are the components shared across these mechanisms? Does the
circuit seem similar across different LMs?
• How do these mechanisms develop over the course of the LM’s training? We can use open source
checkpoints of LMs at different stages of training to identify how these mechanisms come to be.
• Are these same mechanisms used in more naturalistic settings? How can we test this?
• Can we improve these mechanisms in order to improve its long-context reasoning abilities?
• Older models seem to be much worse at this task than current ones, even while having a similar
amount of parameters and training data. What mechanisms do these older models rely on, what
makes them worse, and why do they develop those mechanisms as opposed to the ones we see
present in all other current LMs?
Our objective is to deepen our understanding of how models implement entity binding and how they use
this for reasoning by approaching the problem from several complementary directions: identifying the
underlying circuits, tracing their emergence during training, and testing their generality across models
and contexts. Students are welcome to focus on any one of these directions or propose a related angle of
research.
